# Copy to deploy.env (not committed) and fill in. Used by scripts/deploy.sh.

# Where the bot runs: an ssh destination you can log into as root. One
# deploy.env runs one config; for another server, use another DIR and SERVICE.
HOST=root@my-server
# Install directory on the server.
DIR=/opt/discord-bot
# systemd service name, and the system user it runs as.
SERVICE=discord-bot
# Your community's config repo (ssh URL). The server gets its own deploy key
# for it.
CONFIG_REPO=git@github.com:my-org/my-config.git
CONFIG_BRANCH=main
# Optional: owner/repo, so `setup` can add the deploy key with the GitHub CLI.
CONFIG_REPO_GH=my-org/my-config
# The file uploaded as the server's .env by `deploy.sh secrets` (which then
# restarts the bot). What goes in it: docs/deploy.md, Settings. It's the
# only copy of the bot's secrets: keep it in your password manager too.
SECRETS=.env.server

# Optional web UI (docs/deploy.md: Web dashboard over Tailscale). Needs
# Tailscale on HOST and DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET in the secrets file.
# WEB_URL=https://my-server.my-tailnet.ts.net
# WEB_PORT=8080

# Optional: after a restart, seconds to wait for the bot to say it connected,
# then to watch it stays up. If it doesn't, deploy switches back and fails.
# HEALTH_WAIT=30
# HEALTH_STABLE=30
# Set SKIP_TESTS=1 (here or when running deploy.sh) to deploy without running
# the tests locally first.

# Optional: where releases are built (docs/deploy.md, Where releases are
# built). server: on HOST, as its own unprivileged user (SERVICE-build, with
# its own Rust in DIR/build), never as root; needs about 2 GB of memory and
# 5 GB of disk for it. local: here, then copied over and checked by hash;
# this machine's system must be no newer than the server's (its glibc).
# DEPLOY_BUILD=server
# cargo's -j: fewer parallel jobs use less memory (default: every CPU).
# BUILD_JOBS=2

# Optional: git tag moved to the running release, for the config repo's CI
# (ref: deployed). Empty: none. Use deployed-<service> if one repo deploys
# several bots.
# DEPLOY_TAG=deployed
# DEPLOY_TAG_REMOTE=origin

# Optional: nightly encrypted off-site backups (`deploy.sh backup-setup`;
# docs/deploy.md, Backups). Each night the server bundles the database, its
# .env and a note of the config commit, encrypts that to your age public key
# (age-keygen makes one; the private key never goes on the server), signs it
# with a key of its own and sends it to BACKUP_TARGET:
# - rclone:REMOTE:PATH;
# - USER@HOST:PATH, over ssh with a key of the server's own, which may only
#   add, list, read and prune bundles there (deploy/backup-dest.sh); PATH is
#   relative to that account's home (quote a ~/: 'nas@home:~/backups');
# - pull: it stays on the server until `deploy.sh backup --latest` here.
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...
# BACKUP_TARGET=pull
# BACKUP_KEEP=30
# 1: each bundle holds the voice recordings too (else a list of them).
# BACKUP_RECORDINGS=1
# When (systemd OnCalendar; default daily), quoted; a random hour's delay is
# added.
# BACKUP_CALENDAR='*-*-* 04:00'
# On this machine: your age private key file, for restore and backup-check.
# BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY=$HOME/.config/age/discord-bot.key
# backup-check says a backup is stale after this many hours.
# BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=26
# The server signs its bundles; backup-setup shows its key and offers to add
# this. restore and backup-check trust only bundles it signed.
# BACKUP_SIGNING_KEY='ssh-ed25519 AAAA...'
# Settings a restored .env may hold besides those the bot is known to use
# (docs/deploy.md, Backups), space-separated.
# BACKUP_ENV_KEYS='MY_PARTNER_WEBHOOK'

# Optional: a check every minute that the bot is up (`deploy.sh monitor-setup`;
# docs/deploy.md, Watching that it's up). The URLs are secrets, so they go in
# the secrets file, not here: MONITOR_WEBHOOK (a Discord webhook in a channel
# the committee reads) and/or MONITOR_PING_URL (an outside check such as
# healthchecks.io, which also notices the whole server going down).
# Minutes without a heartbeat before it says the bot is down, and hours
# before it says so again (0: once).
# MONITOR_STALE_MINUTES=5
# MONITOR_REPEAT_HOURS=6
# Also by mail (needs a working sendmail on the server, such as msmtp).
# MONITOR_EMAIL=committee@example.org
# A role the webhook's message pings (its id).
# MONITOR_ROLE=123456789012345678
